WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.700
Authorization decides whether an authenticated identity may perform a specific action on a specific resource under the current conditions.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:21.800
In ASP.NET Core Web APIs, that decision may appear on controllers, actions, minimal APIs, route groups, endpoint filters, service-layer methods, or resource-based authorization handlers.

3
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:28.300
Whatever pattern the application uses, the rule should be applied consistently wherever the operation can occur.

4
00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:34.500
Claims and scopes are important inputs, but they are not complete access decisions by themselves.

5
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:45.000
A scope may say that a client can request invoices, but the API still has to decide which invoices, for which tenant, under which role, and in which workflow state.

6
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:51.900
A claim may describe a user or service, but local business rules decide how that claim maps to allowed behavior.

7
00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:55.860
Policy-based authorization helps make rules explicit.

8
00:00:55.960 --> 00:01:03.800
Policies can combine roles, claims, scopes, tenant context, ownership, operation type, environment, and resource attributes.

9
00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:13.820
Role-based authorization can still be useful when roles match real responsibilities, but complex API decisions often need policies that reflect business rules rather than broad labels.

10
00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.620
Object-level authorization is essential for APIs.

11
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:21.480
A user may be allowed to view reports, but not every report.

12
00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:26.060
A partner integration may submit jobs for one account, but not another.

13
00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:32.560
A background worker may update a processing state, but not change ownership or approval.

14
00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:41.540
Authorization should include the actual record, tenant, file, job, administrative action, or data set when that context matters.

15
00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:52.380
Least privilege applies to all callers: humans, service accounts, administrators, background workers, partner integrations, and deployment systems.

16
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.640
API credentials should not be broader than the integration needs.

17
00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:05.860
Administrative and support APIs are especially high-risk because they often combine broad data visibility with powerful actions and urgent operational workflows.

18
00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:10.300
Centralized, reviewable authorization logic reduces drift.

19
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.800
If each endpoint invents its own permission check, rules become inconsistent and hard to audit.

20
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:29.620
Well-named policies, resource-based handlers, shared application services, and clear tests make it easier for code reviewers and security teams to understand the real access model.
